WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.200
Spider-Man is swinging back into the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, and it's all because of...

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:05.880
you.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.580
Disney co-chairman Alan Horn and Sony Pictures
chairman Tom Rothman recently took part in

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.110
a roundtable discussion in which they agreed
that if not for pushback from fans, this summer's

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:20.560
split which temporarily saw the wall-crawler
ousted from the MCU might have remained permanent.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.920
If you'll recall, Horn and Rothman managed
to hammer out the finer points of a deal which

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.200
will allow Tom Holland's Peter Parker to appear
in at least one more solo film set in the

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.310
MCU, as well as one additional, as-yet unnamed
Marvel Studios picture.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:38.110
When it was reported in August that Sony and
Disney, Marvel's parent company, had failed

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.570
to reach an agreement to extend their shared
custody arrangement of ol' web-head, fan backlash

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:47.239
was instant and furious and this, said Horn,
was ultimately what convinced the involved

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.060
parties to get a deal done.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.000
Horn explained:

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.080
"The fan base, which is important to all of
us, seemed to really respond to what Tom and

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.020
his folks have done before with our people.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.899
They like the fact that the Marvel Cinematic
Universe and [Marvel Studios head honcho]

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.960
Kevin Feige were involved [in Spider-Man:
Homecoming and Spider-Man: Far From Home].

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.300
We heard feedback out there that suggested
that joining forces once again was probably

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.600
really a good idea."

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.880
"Why don't you try that on, and I'll introduce
the world to the newest official member of

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:15.880
the Avengers…

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:16.780
Spider-Man."

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.660
Horn, apparently, is a master of understatement.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.140
Twitter absolutely exploded in the wake of
the news that Sony and Marvel were divorcing,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.660
with a large chunk of fans simply beside themselves
with shock and grief.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.259
Holland himself did his best to console them
during an appearance at Disney's D23 expo

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:36.439
the following weekend, and it may have been
during that event that the young star upon

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:40.950
seeing just how much his version of the character
meant to Marvel's fans decided upon a course

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.960
of action that few in his position would be
gutsy enough to take.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.920
He reportedly got in touch with Disney CEO
Bob Iger one of the most powerful people in

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.290
the world of entertainment and told him in
no uncertain terms that he needed to get Sony

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:54.380
back to the table.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.340
Then, he allegedly put in a call to Rothman
to tell him the same thing.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.820
Improbably, the ploy didn't backfire ridiculously.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.460
Holland kept his job, the two sides got back
together, a deal was struck, and the fans

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.240
rejoiced.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.580
Spider-Man is back in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, but now, fans have another question:

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.440
for how long?

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.120
Are Spidey's days really numbered?

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.500
"You are a criminal!

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.240
A menace!

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.540
And the good people of this nation will squash
you like the bug you are!"

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.760
Whether the deal will continue after Spidey
completes his MCU solo film and team-up appearance

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.849
is a giant question mark, but given the fact
that there are tons and tons of box office

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:33.240
bucks to be made by keeping fans happy, we
tend to think that it's more likely than not.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.680
Of course, the question was put to Rothman
during THR's roundtable, and while he didn't

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:42.580
enthusiastically answer in the affirmative,
he also didn't say no and it's pretty obvious

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.000
that he recognizes the value of the partnership.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.420
The query was framed in an odd way, with the
interviewer mentioning that Sony has, quote,

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.900
"shown [it] can do Spider-Man without Marvel"
by way of the Oscar-winning animated film

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.520
Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:56.840
Rothman responded:

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.020
"Yes, we have.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.300
But I agree with [Horn].

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.000
This was a classic win-win-win.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.900
A win for Sony, a win for Disney, a win for
the fans.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.360
The only thing I would say is that news cycles
and the rhythm of negotiations do not necessarily

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.220
overlap.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.200
And this is, in the words of Shakespeare,
a consummation devoutly to be wished.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.900
We would have gotten there, and the news got
ahead of some things."

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.460
There's actually quite a bit to unpack in
that statement, not the least of which is

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:23.820
Rothman's apparent Shakespeare fandom and
his seemingly disingenuous suggestion that

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.230
the media got ahead of the negotiations, as
most details that was being reported was coming

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.820
directly out of the mouths of the involved
parties.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:36.800
In September, Sony CEO Tony Vinciquerra went
so far as to say the, quote, "door was closed"

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.740
on Spidey's return to the MCU.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.360
He said, at the time:

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.410
"Spider-Man was fine before the event movies,
did better with the event movies, and now

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.530
that we have our own universe, he will play
off the other characters as well.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.670
I think we're pretty capable of doing what
we have to do here."

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:55.159
Thankfully, the fan backlash and Holland's
timely intervention pushed the studios back

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:59.370
together, and now that they're back in business,
it seems unlikely that anyone involved will

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.180
be keen to go through such a trying public
ordeal again.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.520
Feige has in the past teased his willingness
to consider crossing over or even merging

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:11.459
Sony's budding cinematic universe with the
MCU, because even before the whole kerfuffle,

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:15.959
he knew what Iger, Horn, and Rothman had to
learn the hard way: Spider-fans like their

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.840
web-slinger right where he is.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:18.840
"What are you doing?"

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.260
"Oh, this is nice."

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.700
Spidey's as-yet untitled third MCU solo outing
hits the big screen on July 16, 2021.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.820
Fingers crossed that it delivers on that Far
From Home cliffhanger.

